# libnl -- the netlink library suite.
#
# WHY IT IS IN THIS DISTRIBUTION, WHICH IS NOT WHAT THE NAME SUGGESTS.
#
# The obvious answer -- "NetworkManager needs it" -- IS WRONG, and it was worth
# ten minutes to find out. NetworkManager 1.54.0 contains ZERO references to
# libnl anywhere in its tree: it carries its own netlink implementation under
# src/libnm-platform and takes no such dependency in meson.build. Older
# NetworkManager did link it, which is why the belief outlives the fact.
#
# The real consumer is WPA_SUPPLICANT, whose nl80211 driver is how any Wi-Fi
# actually happens. src/drivers/drivers.mak sets NEED_LIBNL=y and
# CONFIG_LIBNL3_ROUTE=y for that driver and links libnl-3.0, libnl-genl-3.0 and
# libnl-route-3.0. NetworkManager then drives wpa_supplicant over D-Bus.
#
# So the chain that makes gnome-control-center's Wi-Fi panel do anything is
#     libnl -> wpa_supplicant -> (D-Bus) -> NetworkManager -> libnm -> panel
# and this package is the bottom of it, not a NetworkManager dependency.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/thom311/libnl/releases/download/libnl3_11_0/libnl-3.11.0.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=2a56e1edefa3e68a7c00879496736fdbf62fc94ed3232c0baba127ecfa76874d
SRC_DIR=libnl-3.11.0

# --sysconfdir=/etc rather than the default /usr/etc. libnl reads
# $(sysconfdir)/libnl/classid and pktloc at RUNTIME, and the path is baked into
# the library at compile time by -D_NL_SYSCONFDIR_LIBNL (Makefile.am line 48).
# Left at the default, the files install to /usr/etc/libnl and the library looks
# for them there -- consistent, wrong, and invisible until something asks for a
# traffic-control class by name.
#
# --disable-static: nothing here links statically, and the .a files are a third
# of the payload.
#
# --enable-cli IS LEFT AT ITS DEFAULT OF "bin", which installs the nl-* tools
# into /usr/bin along with libnl-cli-3 and its dlopened modules under
# /usr/lib/libnl/cli/. That is BLFS's configuration and it is kept deliberately:
# on a machine whose Wi-Fi is not working, nl-link-list and nl-addr-list are the
# only way to ask the kernel what it thinks the interfaces are without a working
# NetworkManager. The alternative values are "sbin", "no-inst" and "no"
# (configure.ac lines 100-108); the default is recorded here rather than passed,
# for the same reason as nghttp2's --disable-examples.
#
# NOT PASSED, and each would resolve to off today for a reason that could change:
#   --disable-pthreads   pthreads is in glibc; leaving it on costs nothing.
#   --enable-debug       defaults off already.
# The unit-test suite needs `check`, which is not packaged; configure's
# PKG_CHECK_MODULES for it is guarded and simply skips the tests.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--sysconfdir=/etc --disable-static"
